    <dcterms:title xml:lang="en">Classification of digital health interventions v1.0: a shared language to describe the uses of digital technology for health</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:creator>World Health Organization</dcterms:creator>
    <dcterms:issued xsi:type="dcterms:W3CDTF">2018-03-14</dcterms:issued>
    <dcterms:publisher>World Health Organization</dcterms:publisher>
    <dcterms:identifier xsi:type="dcterms:URI">https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/260480</dcterms:identifier>
    <dcterms:language xsi:type="dcterms:RFC4646">en</dcterms:language>
    <dcterms:abstract xml:lang="en">This publication presents version 1.0 of the World Health Organization’s Classification of Digital Health Interventions (DHIs), a taxonomy developed to provide a shared and standardized language for describing how digital and mobile technologies are used to address health system needs. Intended primarily for public health audiences, the framework supports communication among policymakers, programme planners, implementers, researchers, donors and technology specialists by defining digital health interventions as discrete digital functionalities linked to specific health objectives. The classification was developed to facilitate evidence synthesis, landscape analyses, planning processes and the articulation of digital solutions to identified health system challenges.
  
  The framework organizes digital health interventions into four primary groups according to the main user: clients, health-care providers, health system managers and data services. It links digital interventions to broader health system challenges and to system categories such as electronic medical records, logistics management information systems and telemedicine platforms. Developed through technical consultations, literature review and public feedback, the classification builds on existing mHealth and eHealth frameworks while providing a simplified and practical reference for health programmes. The document is intended to support the design, implementation, evaluation and interoperability of digital health initiatives and will be periodically updated to reflect emerging technologies and evolving practices in digital health.</dcterms:abstract>
    <dcterms:type>report</dcterms:type>
  </metadata>
